@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1461 = private unnamed_addr constant [26 x i8] c"start-process-with-a-shell", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1462 = private unnamed_addr constant [1202 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for calls that start a process with a shell, providing guidance on\0Awhether the usage is safe or not.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AStarting a process with a shell can introduce security risks, such as\0Acode injection vulnerabilities. It's important to be aware of whether the\0Ausage of the shell is safe or not.\0A\0AThis rule triggers on functions like `os.system`, `popen`, etc., which\0Astart processes with a shell. It evaluates whether the provided command\0Ais a literal string or an expression. If the command is a literal string,\0Ait's considered safe. If the command is an expression, it's considered\0A(potentially) unsafe.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0A# Safe usage (literal string)\0Acommand = \22ls -l\22\0Aos.system(command)\0A\0A# Potentially unsafe usage (expression)\0Acmd = get_user_input()\0Aos.system(cmd)\0A```\0A\0A## Note\0AThe `subprocess` module provides more powerful facilities for spawning new\0Aprocesses and retrieving their results, and using that module is preferable\0Ato using `os.system` or similar functions. Consider replacing such usages\0Awith `subprocess.call` or related functions.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\0A", align 1
