@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1478 = private unnamed_addr constant [30 x i8] c"unnecessary-call-around-sorted", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1479 = private unnamed_addr constant [938 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `list()` or `reversed()` calls around `sorted()`\0Acalls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt is unnecessary to use `list()` around `sorted()`, as the latter already\0Areturns a list.\0A\0AIt is also unnecessary to use `reversed()` around `sorted()`, as the latter\0Ahas a `reverse` argument that can be used in lieu of an additional\0A`reversed()` call.\0A\0AIn both cases, it's clearer and more efficient to avoid the redundant call.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Areversed(sorted(iterable))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Asorted(iterable, reverse=True)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as `reversed()` and `reverse=True` will\0Ayield different results in the event of custom sort keys or equality\0Afunctions. Specifically, `reversed()` will reverse the order of the\0Acollection, while `sorted()` with `reverse=True` will perform a stable\0Areverse sort, which will preserve the order of elements that compare as\0Aequal.\0A", align 1
