@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1490 = private unnamed_addr constant [1372 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `datetime.datetime.strptime()` that lead to naive\0Adatetime objects.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython datetime objects can be naive or timezone-aware. While an aware\0Aobject represents a specific moment in time, a naive object does not\0Acontain enough information to unambiguously locate itself relative to other\0Adatetime objects. Since this can lead to errors, it is recommended to\0Aalways use timezone-aware objects.\0A\0A`datetime.datetime.strptime()` without `%z` returns a naive datetime\0Aobject. Follow it with `.replace(tzinfo=<timezone>)` or `.astimezone()`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.strptime(\222022/01/31\22, \22%Y/%m/%d\22)\0A```\0A\0AInstead, use `.replace(tzinfo=<timezone>)`:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.strptime(\222022/01/31\22, \22%Y/%m/%d\22).replace(\0A    tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc\0A)\0A```\0A\0AOr, use `.astimezone()`:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.strptime(\222022/01/31\22, \22%Y/%m/%d\22).astimezone(datetime.timezone.utc)\0A```\0A\0AOn Python 3.11 and later, `datetime.timezone.utc` can be replaced with\0A`datetime.UTC`.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Aware and Naive Objects](https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#aware-and-naive-objects)\0A- [Python documentation: `strftime()` and `strptime()` Behavior](https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#strftime-and-strptime-behavior)\0A", align 1
