@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1520 = private unnamed_addr constant [37 x i8] c"django-non-leading-receiver-decorator", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1521 = private unnamed_addr constant [861 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that Django's `@receiver` decorator is listed first, prior to\0Aany other decorators.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ADjango's `@receiver` decorator is special in that it does not return\0Aa wrapped function. Rather, `@receiver` connects the decorated function\0Ato a signal. If any other decorators are listed before `@receiver`,\0Athe decorated function will not be connected to the signal.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom django.dispatch import receiver\0Afrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\0A\0A\0A@transaction.atomic\0A@receiver(post_save, sender=MyModel)\0Adef my_handler(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom django.dispatch import receiver\0Afrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\0A\0A\0A@receiver(post_save, sender=MyModel)\0A@transaction.atomic\0Adef my_handler(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\0A    pass\0A```\0A", align 1
