@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1529 = private unnamed_addr constant [951 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of f-strings in exception constructors.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython includes the `raise` in the default traceback (and formatters\0Alike Rich and IPython do too).\0A\0ABy using an f-string, the error message will be duplicated in the\0Atraceback, which can make the traceback less readable.\0A\0A## Example\0AGiven:\0A```python\0Asub = \22Some value\22\0Araise RuntimeError(f\22{sub!r} is incorrect\22)\0A```\0A\0APython will produce a traceback like:\0A```console\0ATraceback (most recent call last):\0A  File \22tmp.py\22, line 2, in <module>\0A    raise RuntimeError(f\22{sub!r} is incorrect\22)\0ARuntimeError: 'Some value' is incorrect\0A```\0A\0AInstead, assign the string to a variable:\0A```python\0Asub = \22Some value\22\0Amsg = f\22{sub!r} is incorrect\22\0Araise RuntimeError(msg)\0A```\0A\0AWhich will produce a traceback like:\0A```console\0ATraceback (most recent call last):\0A  File \22tmp.py\22, line 3, in <module>\0A    raise RuntimeError(msg)\0ARuntimeError: 'Some value' is incorrect\0A```\0A", align 1
