@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1548 = private unnamed_addr constant [828 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `pprint` statements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ALike `print` statements, `pprint` statements used for debugging should\0Abe omitted from production code. They can lead the accidental inclusion\0Aof sensitive information in logs, and are not configurable by clients,\0Aunlike `logging` statements.\0A\0A`pprint` statements used to produce output as a part of a command-line\0Ainterface program are not typically a problem.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pprint\0A\0A\0Adef merge_dicts(dict_a, dict_b):\0A    dict_c = {**dict_a, **dict_b}\0A    pprint.pprint(dict_c)\0A    return dict_c\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef merge_dicts(dict_a, dict_b):\0A    dict_c = {**dict_a, **dict_b}\0A    return dict_c\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it will remove `pprint` statements\0Athat are used beyond debugging purposes.\0A", align 1
