@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1562 = private unnamed_addr constant [993 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary parentheses on raised exceptions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf an exception is raised without any arguments, parentheses are not\0Arequired, as the `raise` statement accepts either an exception instance\0Aor an exception class (which is then implicitly instantiated).\0A\0ARemoving the parentheses makes the code more concise.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AParentheses can only be omitted if the exception is a class, as opposed to\0Aa function call. This rule isn't always capable of distinguishing between\0Athe two.\0A\0AFor example, if you import a function `module.get_exception` from another\0Amodule, and `module.get_exception` returns an exception object, this rule will\0Aincorrectly mark the parentheses in `raise module.get_exception()` as\0Aunnecessary.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Araise TypeError()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Araise TypeError\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `raise` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-raise-statement)\0A", align 1
