@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1572 = private unnamed_addr constant [1433 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `.split(os.sep)`\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `pathlib` module in the standard library should be used for path\0Amanipulation. It provides a high-level API with the functionality\0Aneeded for common operations on `Path` objects.\0A\0A## Example\0AIf not all parts of the path are needed, then the `name` and `parent`\0Aattributes of the `Path` object should be used. Otherwise, the `parts`\0Aattribute can be used as shown in the last example.\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0A\22path/to/file_name.txt\22.split(os.sep)[-1]\0A\0A\22path/to/file_name.txt\22.split(os.sep)[-2]\0A\0A# Iterating over the path parts\0Aif any(part in blocklist for part in \22my/file/path\22.split(os.sep)):\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pathlib import Path\0A\0APath(\22path/to/file_name.txt\22).name\0A\0APath(\22path/to/file_name.txt\22).parent.name\0A\0A# Iterating over the path parts\0Aif any(part in blocklist for part in Path(\22my/file/path\22).parts):\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AWhile using `pathlib` can improve the readability and type safety of your code,\0Ait can be less performant than working directly with strings,\0Aespecially on older versions of Python.\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 428 \E2\80\93 The pathlib module \E2\80\93 object-oriented filesystem paths](https://peps.python.org/pep-0428/)\0A- [Why you should be using pathlib](https://treyhunner.com/2018/12/why-you-should-be-using-pathlib/)\0A- [No really, pathlib is great](https://treyhunner.com/2019/01/no-really-pathlib-is-great/)\0A", align 1
