@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1584 = private unnamed_addr constant [1398 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `os.makedirs`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`pathlib` offers a high-level API for path manipulation, as compared to\0Athe lower-level API offered by `os`. When possible, using `Path` object\0Amethods such as `Path.mkdir(parents=True)` can improve readability over the\0A`os` module's counterparts (e.g., `os.makedirs()`.\0A\0A## Examples\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0Aos.makedirs(\22./nested/directory/\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pathlib import Path\0A\0APath(\22./nested/directory/\22).mkdir(parents=True)\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AWhile using `pathlib` can improve the readability and type safety of your code,\0Ait can be less performant than the lower-level alternatives that work directly with strings,\0Aespecially on older versions of Python.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `Path.mkdir`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.mkdir)\0A- [Python documentation: `os.makedirs`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.makedirs)\0A- [PEP 428 \E2\80\93 The pathlib module \E2\80\93 object-oriented filesystem paths](https://peps.python.org/pep-0428/)\0A- [Correspondence between `os` and `pathlib`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#correspondence-to-tools-in-the-os-module)\0A- [Why you should be using pathlib](https://treyhunner.com/2018/12/why-you-should-be-using-pathlib/)\0A- [No really, pathlib is great](https://treyhunner.com/2019/01/no-really-pathlib-is-great/)\0A", align 1
