@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1674 = private unnamed_addr constant [1256 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `open()` builtin.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`pathlib` offers a high-level API for path manipulation. When possible,\0Ausing `Path` object methods such as `Path.open()` can improve readability\0Aover the `open` builtin.\0A\0A## Examples\0A```python\0Awith open(\22f1.py\22, \22wb\22) as fp:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pathlib import Path\0A\0Awith Path(\22f1.py\22).open(\22wb\22) as fp:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AWhile using `pathlib` can improve the readability and type safety of your code,\0Ait can be less performant than working directly with strings,\0Aespecially on older versions of Python.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `Path.open`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.open)\0A- [Python documentation: `open`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#open)\0A- [PEP 428 \E2\80\93 The pathlib module \E2\80\93 object-oriented filesystem paths](https://peps.python.org/pep-0428/)\0A- [Correspondence between `os` and `pathlib`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html#correspondence-to-tools-in-the-os-module)\0A- [Why you should be using pathlib](https://treyhunner.com/2018/12/why-you-should-be-using-pathlib/)\0A- [No really, pathlib is great](https://treyhunner.com/2019/01/no-really-pathlib-is-great/)\0A", align 1
