@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1735 = private unnamed_addr constant [667 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of the characters 'l', 'O', or 'I' as variable names.\0A\0ANote: This rule is automatically disabled for all stub files\0A(files with `.pyi` extensions). The rule has little relevance for authors\0Aof stubs: a well-written stub should aim to faithfully represent the\0Ainterface of the equivalent .py file as it exists at runtime, including any\0Aambiguously named variables in the runtime module.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn some fonts, these characters are indistinguishable from the\0Anumerals one and zero. When tempted to use 'l', use 'L' instead.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Al = 0\0AO = 123\0AI = 42\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0AL = 0\0Ao = 123\0Ai = 42\0A```\0A\0A", align 1
