@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1798 = private unnamed_addr constant [1106 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for f-strings that do not contain any placeholder expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0Af-strings are a convenient way to format strings, but they are not\0Anecessary if there are no placeholder expressions to format. In this\0Acase, a regular string should be used instead, as an f-string without\0Aplaceholders can be confusing for readers, who may expect such a\0Aplaceholder to be present.\0A\0AAn f-string without any placeholders could also indicate that the\0Aauthor forgot to add a placeholder expression.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Af\22Hello, world!\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A\22Hello, world!\22\0A```\0A\0A**Note:** to maintain compatibility with PyFlakes, this rule only flags\0Af-strings that are part of an implicit concatenation if _none_ of the\0Af-string segments contain placeholder expressions.\0A\0AFor example:\0A\0A```python\0A# Will not be flagged.\0A(\0A    f\22Hello,\22\0A    f\22 {name}!\22\0A)\0A\0A# Will be flagged.\0A(\0A    f\22Hello,\22\0A    f\22 World!\22\0A)\0A```\0A\0ASee [#10885](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/10885) for more.\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 498 \E2\80\93 Literal String Interpolation](https://peps.python.org/pep-0498/)\0A", align 1
