@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1887 = private unnamed_addr constant [785 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `int` conversions of values that are already integers.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASuch a conversion is unnecessary.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule may produce false positives for `round`, `math.ceil`, `math.floor`,\0Aand `math.trunc` calls when values override the `__round__`, `__ceil__`, `__floor__`,\0Aor `__trunc__` operators such that they don't return an integer.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aint(len([]))\0Aint(round(foo, None))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Alen([])\0Around(foo)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix for `round`, `math.ceil`, `math.floor`, and `math.truncate` is unsafe\0Abecause removing the `int` conversion can change the semantics for values\0Aoverriding the `__round__`, `__ceil__`, `__floor__`, or `__trunc__` dunder methods\0Asuch that they don't return an integer.\0A", align 1
